{"path":"error/error_code/operator_bool","title":"std::error_code::operator bool","titleHtml":"<span class=\"t-title-prefix\">std::error_code::</span>operator bool","originalUrl":"https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/error/error_code/operator_bool","section":"error","kind":"page","summary":"Checks if the error code value is valid, i.e. non-zero.","html":"<table class=\"t-dcl-begin\"><tbody>\n<tr class=\"t-dcl t-since-cxx11\">\n<td> <div><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\"><span class=\"kw1\">explicit</span> operator <span class=\"kw4\">bool</span><span class=\"br0\">(</span><span class=\"br0\">)</span> <span class=\"kw4\">const</span> <span class=\"kw1\">noexcept</span><span class=\"sy4\">;</span></span></div></td>\n<td class=\"t-dcl-nopad\">  </td>\n<td> <span class=\"t-mark-rev t-since-cxx11\">(since C++11)</span> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"t-dcl-sep\"><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Checks if the error code value is valid, i.e. non-zero.\n</p>\n<h3 id=\"Parameters\">Parameters</h3>\n<p>(none)\n</p>\n<h3 id=\"Return_value\">Return value</h3>\n<p><code class=\"t-c\"><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\"><span class=\"kw2\">false</span></span></code> if <code class=\"t-c\"><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\">value<span class=\"br0\">(</span><span class=\"br0\">)</span> <span class=\"sy1\">==</span> <span class=\"nu0\">0</span></span></code>, <code class=\"t-c\"><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\"><span class=\"kw2\">true</span></span></code> otherwise.\n</p>\n<h3 id=\"Notes\">Notes</h3>\n<p>Although this operator is often used as a convenient shorthand to check if any error was returned, as in <code class=\"t-c\"><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\"><span class=\"kw1\">if</span> <span class=\"br0\">(</span>ec<span class=\"br0\">)</span> <span class=\"br0\">{</span> <span class=\"coMULTI\">/* handle error */</span> <span class=\"br0\">}</span></span></code>, such use is not robust: some error codes, for example, HTTP status code <code class=\"t-c\"><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\"><span class=\"nu0\">200</span></span></code>, may indicate success as well.\n</p>","headings":[{"depth":3,"text":"Parameters","id":"Parameters"},{"depth":3,"text":"Return value","id":"Return_value"},{"depth":3,"text":"Notes","id":"Notes"}],"breadcrumbs":[{"path":"","title":"C++ reference"},{"path":"error","title":"Diagnostics library"},{"path":"error/error_code","title":"std::error_code"}],"children":[],"map":{"label":"std::error_code::operator bool","children":[{"label":"Declarations","note":"1 declaration; revisions: since C++11. Checks if the error code value is valid, i.e. non-zero."},{"label":"Parameters","note":"(none)"},{"label":"Return value","note":"false if value() == 0, true otherwise."},{"label":"Notes","note":"Although this operator is often used as a convenient shorthand to check if any error was returned, as in if (ec) { /* handle error */ }, such use is not…"}]},"standards":["C++11"],"wordCount":75,"codeBlockCount":0}
